{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And if an altar of stones.</b> Rabbi Yishmael says: every אִם in the Torah is optional except three — ״וְאִם מִזְבַּח אֲבָנִים תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי״ — this אִם functions in the sense of כַּאֲשֶׁר: and when you make Me an altar of stones, <b>you shall not build them of hewn stones</b> — for it is an obligation upon you to build an altar of stones, as it says ״אֲבָנִים שְׁלֵמוֹת תִּבְנֶה״ (Deuteronomy 27). And likewise ״וְאִם כֶּסֶף תַּלְוֶה״ (Exodus 22) — it is an obligation, as it says ״וְהַעֲבֵט תַּעֲבִיטֶנּוּ״ (Deuteronomy 15), and this too functions in the sense of כַּאֲשֶׁר. And likewise ״וְאִם תַּקְרִיב מִנְחַת בִּכּוּרִים״ (Leviticus 2) — this is the meal-offering of the omer, which is an obligation; and perforce these אִם's are not conditional but certain, and they function in the sense of כַּאֲשֶׁר." "<b>Hewn.</b> A term of cutting — that one shapes and hews them with iron." "<b>For you have lifted your sword upon it.</b> This כִּי functions in the sense of פֶּן, which is דִּילְמָא — lest you lift your sword upon it." "<b>And you have profaned it.</b> You have learned from this: if you lifted iron upon it, you profaned it — for the altar was created to lengthen a person's days, and iron was created to shorten a person's days; it is not fitting that the shortener be lifted over the lengthener (Mekhilta). And furthermore, the altar brings peace between Israel and their Father in heaven; therefore there shall not come upon it anything that cuts and destroys — and so it is a kal va-chomer: if concerning stones, which do not see nor hear nor speak, because they bring peace the Torah said ״you shall not lift iron upon them,״ then the one who brings peace between a man and his wife, between family and family, between a person and his fellow — all the more so shall no punishment come upon him (there)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:27.812154368Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}